        - [Basal Cell Carcinoma Standard]: Basal cell carcinomas typically exhibit a diverse array of refined features, yet several key visual characteristics consistently emerge. These include nodules and papules with rough, scaly surfaces, exhibiting fine scaling and telangiectatic vessels. Macules may display a pink to reddish hue, along with superficial scaling and fine telangiectatic vessels. Papillations reveal a central area of crusting and superficial scaling, accompanied by surrounding skin alterations like terminal hairs and minimal signs of inflammation or secondary infection. Although individual lesions may vary in size, coloration, and texture, it is crucial to carefully examine and specialized expertise for an accurate diagnosis. Ultimately, basal cell carcinoma follows a predictable pattern with surrounding erythema and mild inflammation, albeit each lesion may present unique variations.
- [Squamous Cell Carcinoma Standard]: Squamous cell carcinomas exhibit a diverse array of morphological features, including well-circumscribed papules, nodules, or plaques with varying colors ranging from erythematous to yellow-brown hues. Central areas commonly demonstrate hyperkeratosis, crusted debris, or ulcerations, while surrounding skin displays diffuse erythema, edema, and telangiectasia. Irregular borders and lack of symmetry characterize these lesions, which can manifest on sun-exposed skin. Histopathologically, SCC displays various internal structures, including regression scars, pigment networks, and globules, depending on disease progression and stage. In summary, squamous cell carcinomas exhibit a complex, evolving morphology with potential for radial symmetry disruption and internal structural variations.
- [Melanoma Standard]: Melanoma, a malignant skin cancer, often manifests as dark, asymmetrical skin lesions with irregular borders and varying pigment distributions. These moles may exhibit colors like brown, black, blue-gray, and occasionally lack uniformity. They might display rough, scaly surfaces and exhibit scaling, crusting, or ulceration. However, they generally do not demonstrate atrophy, lichenification, or radial symmetry. It is essential to monitor these symptoms closely and seek medical attention if you observe any changes in existing moles or the development of new ones. When examining melanotic lesions, look for the following key features: - Irregular borders with notching, raggedness, or incomplete outlining - Variable pigmentation with different shades of brown, black, blue-gray, or absent pigmentation - Asymmetry in shape and coloration - An irregular internal structure lacking radial symmetry or discernible pigment networks, globules, or regression areas - Possible scaling, crusting, ulceration, or telangiectasia on the surface - No apparent inflammation or secondary changes in the surrounding skin - Blue-purple ink outlines applied for documentation or tracking purposes may obscure fine details of the border. While many melanotic lesions are benign nevi, it is crucial to carefully monitor their evolution and promptly report any concerning changes to a medical professional.
- [Nevus Standard]: Nevi, also known as moles, exhibit diverse groupings of skin lesions showcasing varied shapes, sizes, and colors. These lesions demonstrate uniform or uneven pigmentation, well-defined or uneven borders, smooth or rough surfaces, and lack of scaling, crusting, ulceration, atrophy, lichenification, and telangiectasia. Irregular borders, asymmetric shapes, and the absence of discernible internal structural patterns such as pigment networks, globules, or regression scars are common characteristics among nevi. It is essential to monitor each instance individually and consider clinical correlations and potential biopsies for conclusive diagnoses. Regularly assessing these lesions for any changes in size, shape, or color over time is advised.
- [Actinic Keratosis Standard]: Actinic keratoses are typically identified through their unique visual characteristics. These include facial skin lesions located on periorbital regions due to sun exposure, exhibiting erythematous to hyperpigmented plaques with rough, scaly surfaces and central hyperkeratosis. Other features include well-circumscribed papules, domeshaped papules, and solitary, well-circumscribed papules. Lesions may also present as central areas of crusted, keratinous debris or a central crust. Erythematous papules and plaques with a rough, scaly surface and central hyperkeratosis are also common. Surrounding skin often displays signs of aging, such as fine wrinkles and mild xerosis. Although some lesions demonstrate radial symmetry or pigment networks, globules, or regression areas, these features are generally absent. Overall, these dermatologic findings suggest a non-neoplastic etiology, possibly secondary to sun damage, acne scarring, or mild chronic dermatitis.


